WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 0.2.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:05.000
Opening and securing an exchange account.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.076
By the end, you'll open an account

00:00:07.076 --> 00:00:09.270
on a reputable exchange, and lock it

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.405
down before a single dollar goes in.

00:00:12.705 --> 00:00:15.610
Here's why this lesson comes before you buy anything.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.679
The single biggest reason a beginner's first crypto

00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:21.340
experience goes wrong isn't a bad trade.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.754
It's an account that was funded before it

00:00:23.754 --> 00:00:26.456
was secured: a weak password, no two-factor, a

00:00:26.456 --> 00:00:28.710
phone number that can be hijacked.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.415
Every step in this lesson closes one of those

00:00:31.415 --> 00:00:34.120
doors, in order, before money is ever at risk.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.710
Here's the plan.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.852
First, what an exchange actually is,

00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:40.425
and how to choose one.

00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:42.851
Second, the six-step lockdown that comes

00:00:42.851 --> 00:00:44.630
before any money goes in.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.545
Third, Maria's worked example, start to finish.

00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:50.130
And finally, your checklist and a quick quiz.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.595
Here's the idea in one picture.

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.329
An exchange is your on-ramp

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.610
from ordinary money into crypto.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.215
But before you send it a single

00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:01.220
dollar, you lock the account down.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.355
Order matters: account, then security, then money.

00:01:05.655 --> 00:01:06.860
Choosing an exchange.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.415
First, what an exchange even is.

00:01:10.415 --> 00:01:12.535
It's a company that lets you swap

00:01:12.535 --> 00:01:16.015
ordinary money, dollars, pounds, euros, for crypto.

00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:18.755
It's your on-ramp, not your final destination.

00:01:20.055 --> 00:01:23.190
Here's the sign-up sequence you'll actually go through.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.657
You create the account with

00:01:24.657 --> 00:01:26.215
an email and a password.

00:01:26.215 --> 00:01:28.420
You verify that email address.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.239
You complete KYC, uploading your ID

00:01:31.239 --> 00:01:34.124
and sometimes a proof of address.

00:01:34.124 --> 00:01:36.278
And once that's approved, the account

00:01:36.278 --> 00:01:38.279
is ready, but not yet secured.

00:01:38.279 --> 00:01:40.750
That's the part most beginners rush past, and

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.059
it's the whole subject of the next chapter.

00:01:44.359 --> 00:01:47.035
Choose one that's licensed or registered where

00:01:47.035 --> 00:01:50.239
you live, check your country's financial regulator.

00:01:50.239 --> 00:01:53.809
Large and long-established, with a good security record.

00:01:53.809 --> 00:01:57.759
And one that supports your bank's deposit method, plus

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:01.637
the coins and networks you'll actually use, like ETH,

00:02:01.637 --> 00:02:05.744
USDC, and low-cost networks such as Arbitrum or Base.

00:02:05.744 --> 00:02:10.520
Well-known examples include Coinbase, Kraken, Binance, Bybit and

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.194
OKX, but availability differs by country, so pick

00:02:14.194 --> 00:02:16.839
one that's legally available to you.

00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:20.777
You'll also be asked for KYC, know your

00:02:20.777 --> 00:02:24.374
customer: your ID, and sometimes proof of address.

00:02:24.374 --> 00:02:26.416
This is normal, and it's legally

00:02:26.416 --> 00:02:28.819
required for a regulated exchange.

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:30.416
Treat a request for it as a

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.899
good sign, not a red flag.

00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:35.164
So flip those three checks around,

00:02:35.164 --> 00:02:36.924
and you get the red flags.

00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:40.439
A reputable exchange is licensed, has a long track

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:43.471
record, and asks for KYC. A red-flag one is

00:02:43.471 --> 00:02:47.330
unregistered anywhere you can verify, brand new with no

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:52.085
history, and skips identity checks entirely, which sounds convenient

00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:55.324
but is actually a warning sign, not a shortcut.

00:02:56.624 --> 00:02:58.014
One caution, though.

00:02:58.014 --> 00:03:02.494
Choosing a licensed, reputable exchange doesn't mean zero risk.

00:03:02.494 --> 00:03:05.057
Even large, well-known exchanges have

00:03:05.057 --> 00:03:07.717
had outages and security incidents.

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:09.840
It means a far better starting point,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.578
and someone accountable if something goes wrong,

00:03:12.578 --> 00:03:14.757
not a guarantee that nothing ever will.

00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:18.324
One habit protects you before any of

00:03:18.324 --> 00:03:20.960
the lockdown steps even apply: making sure

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.282
you're actually on the real site.

00:03:23.282 --> 00:03:25.355
Type the address yourself, or use a

00:03:25.355 --> 00:03:27.612
bookmark you saved the first time.

00:03:27.612 --> 00:03:29.935
Check the domain matches, letter for letter,

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:32.093
not just something that looks close.

00:03:32.093 --> 00:03:33.583
Look for the padlock.

00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:35.944
And never log in by following a link

00:03:35.944 --> 00:03:38.177
from an email, a text, or a search

00:03:38.177 --> 00:03:40.410
ad, even if it looks exactly right.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.125
The six-step lockdown.

00:03:44.425 --> 00:03:46.492
Here's why the order matters, using

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.515
the first step as the example.

00:03:48.515 --> 00:03:50.599
Skip securing your email, and this

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:52.625
is the chain that can follow.

00:03:52.625 --> 00:03:55.054
Someone breaks into your email, often with

00:03:55.054 --> 00:03:57.895
a password leaked from another site entirely.

00:03:57.895 --> 00:04:00.360
They click forgot password on your exchange.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.769
The reset link lands in

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:03.655
the email they now control.

00:04:03.655 --> 00:04:05.216
And they set a new exchange

00:04:05.216 --> 00:04:07.355
password, before you even notice.

00:04:07.355 --> 00:04:09.113
Lock the email first, and this

00:04:09.113 --> 00:04:10.870
whole chain breaks at step one.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.845
Here's the first half, the must-do basics.

00:04:14.845 --> 00:04:18.408
Step one, secure your email first, because whoever controls

00:04:18.408 --> 00:04:21.380
it can often reset your exchange account too.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.024
Step two, a unique, long password for the

00:04:24.024 --> 00:04:27.220
exchange itself, stored in a password manager.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.610
And step three, two-factor authentication with an authenticator app

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.580
or a hardware security key, not text messages.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.830
Why not text messages?

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.003
Because a criminal can talk your phone company into

00:04:41.003 --> 00:04:43.688
moving your number to their SIM card, a SIM

00:04:43.688 --> 00:04:46.490
swap, and your codes go straight to them.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.498
An authenticator app, or a hardware

00:04:48.498 --> 00:04:50.395
key, never leaves your possession.

00:04:51.695 --> 00:04:54.722
Here's exactly how a SIM swap works, because

00:04:54.722 --> 00:04:57.780
the mechanism is what makes it dangerous.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.893
The attacker calls your phone company, posing as

00:05:00.893 --> 00:05:04.370
you, often using personal details found elsewhere.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.895
They convince the company to move your

00:05:06.895 --> 00:05:09.525
number onto a SIM card they control.

00:05:09.525 --> 00:05:12.960
Your phone loses signal, usually without much warning.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.340
And every text message, including your two-factor codes,

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:18.950
now arrives on their phone instead of yours.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.767
Here's the second half, the

00:05:21.767 --> 00:05:23.855
extra armour most beginners skip.

00:05:23.855 --> 00:05:26.274
Step four, save your two-factor backup

00:05:26.274 --> 00:05:29.055
codes offline, on paper, somewhere safe.

00:05:29.055 --> 00:05:31.811
Step five, turn on the anti-phishing code if

00:05:31.811 --> 00:05:34.444
it's offered, a word that appears in every

00:05:34.444 --> 00:05:37.010
genuine email the exchange sends you.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:39.694
And step six, turn on the withdrawal address

00:05:39.694 --> 00:05:42.735
allowlist if it's offered, so withdrawals can only

00:05:42.735 --> 00:05:45.180
ever go to addresses you've pre-approved.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.570
Quick check.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.845
What does a withdrawal allowlist actually do?

00:05:54.265 --> 00:05:57.258
The answer: it restricts withdrawals so they can

00:05:57.258 --> 00:06:00.311
only go to addresses you've pre-approved, so even

00:06:00.311 --> 00:06:03.365
a hijacked account can't send funds somewhere new.

00:06:04.665 --> 00:06:06.090
Maria's worked example.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:10.325
Here's exactly where Maria's twenty minutes goes.

00:06:10.325 --> 00:06:12.661
Ten minutes on KYC, signing up

00:06:12.661 --> 00:06:14.845
and verifying who she is.

00:06:14.845 --> 00:06:19.181
And ten more on security: a password manager, authenticator-app

00:06:19.181 --> 00:06:22.840
two-factor on both her email and the exchange, backup

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.985
codes on paper, and an anti-phishing code.

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:28.185
Security isn't the slow part.

00:06:28.185 --> 00:06:29.639
It's half the job, and it's

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:31.145
the half most beginners skip.

00:06:32.445 --> 00:06:34.640
Now the whole thing, step by step.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.860
Maria signs up, and completes

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.010
KYC in about ten minutes.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.137
She installs a password manager, and

00:06:41.137 --> 00:06:43.565
gives the exchange a unique password.

00:06:43.565 --> 00:06:46.455
She turns on authenticator-app two-factor, on

00:06:46.455 --> 00:06:48.766
both her email and the exchange.

00:06:48.766 --> 00:06:50.507
She writes her backup codes on

00:06:50.507 --> 00:06:52.491
paper, and stores them safely.

00:06:52.491 --> 00:06:55.666
And she sets an anti-phishing code: the word "blue-kettle."

00:06:56.966 --> 00:06:59.291
Here's why that one word works so well.

00:06:59.291 --> 00:07:01.350
You choose it yourself, when you turn

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.137
the feature on, and only you and

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:04.846
the exchange ever know it.

00:07:04.846 --> 00:07:07.292
Every genuine email the exchange sends you

00:07:07.292 --> 00:07:09.583
includes it, somewhere in the text.

00:07:09.583 --> 00:07:11.846
An attacker writing a fake email has no

00:07:11.846 --> 00:07:14.013
way to know what word you picked.

00:07:14.013 --> 00:07:16.888
So a missing code isn't a minor detail.

00:07:16.888 --> 00:07:17.983
It's the whole tell.

00:07:19.283 --> 00:07:22.465
A few weeks later, an email arrives: “suspicious

00:07:22.465 --> 00:07:25.883
login, verify your account now.” It looks right.

00:07:25.883 --> 00:07:27.898
Same logo, same layout.

00:07:27.898 --> 00:07:30.253
But it doesn't say “blue-kettle” anywhere.

00:07:30.253 --> 00:07:32.303
Maria knows instantly it's fake, and

00:07:32.303 --> 00:07:34.298
deletes it without clicking a thing.

00:07:35.598 --> 00:07:37.609
One more thing, if you already opened

00:07:37.609 --> 00:07:39.973
an exchange account before this lesson.

00:07:39.973 --> 00:07:42.073
Everything here still applies to it.

00:07:42.073 --> 00:07:43.869
Go back now, and work through all

00:07:43.869 --> 00:07:46.141
six lockdown steps on that account, before

00:07:46.141 --> 00:07:47.673
you do anything else with it.

00:07:48.973 --> 00:07:50.153
Checklist and quiz.

00:07:51.453 --> 00:07:53.348
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:07:53.348 --> 00:07:57.598
Exchange chosen: legally available and reputable where you live.

00:07:57.598 --> 00:07:59.403
KYC complete.

00:07:59.403 --> 00:08:02.858
Email secured with its own strong password and two-factor.

00:08:02.858 --> 00:08:07.046
Exchange two-factor via an authenticator app or security

00:08:07.046 --> 00:08:10.578
key, never SMS. Backup codes stored offline.

00:08:10.578 --> 00:08:13.504
And the anti-phishing code and withdrawal allowlist

00:08:13.504 --> 00:08:15.868
switched on, if your exchange offers them.

00:08:17.168 --> 00:08:18.413
Question two.

00:08:18.413 --> 00:08:20.698
Why secure your email before the exchange?

00:08:25.118 --> 00:08:28.628
The answer: email is often how accounts get reset.

00:08:28.628 --> 00:08:30.770
Whoever controls your email can often

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.743
take over the exchange account too.

00:08:34.043 --> 00:08:35.313
Question three. Why

00:08:35.313 --> 00:08:37.618
avoid SMS two-factor? [[pause 4]] The

00:08:42.038 --> 00:08:45.345
answer: attackers can hijack a phone number with a

00:08:45.345 --> 00:08:48.563
SIM swap, and receive your codes directly. An

00:08:48.563 --> 00:08:50.692
authenticator app or hardware key

00:08:50.692 --> 00:08:52.508
can't be redirected that way.

00:08:53.808 --> 00:08:55.063
Question four.

00:08:55.063 --> 00:08:57.109
If a real email from your exchange

00:08:57.109 --> 00:08:59.973
always includes your anti-phishing code, and one

00:08:59.973 --> 00:09:02.253
arrives without it, what should you do?

00:09:06.673 --> 00:09:08.618
The answer: treat it as fake.

00:09:08.618 --> 00:09:10.903
Don't click any link in it, and delete it.

00:09:10.903 --> 00:09:12.408
The missing code is the tell.

00:09:13.708 --> 00:09:16.518
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:16.518 --> 00:09:19.363
You choose a licensed, established exchange.

00:09:19.363 --> 00:09:21.548
You verify you're really on its site.

00:09:21.548 --> 00:09:23.507
You complete KYC. You work

00:09:23.507 --> 00:09:26.057
through all six lockdown steps.

00:09:26.057 --> 00:09:28.092
And only then do you deposit.

00:09:28.092 --> 00:09:29.741
Do those five things, in order,

00:09:29.741 --> 00:09:30.977
and this lesson is done.

00:09:32.277 --> 00:09:33.522
Let's recap.

00:09:33.522 --> 00:09:36.333
Choose an exchange that's licensed, established,

00:09:36.333 --> 00:09:38.182
and supports what you need.

00:09:38.182 --> 00:09:40.592
Complete K.Y.C., it's normal.

00:09:40.592 --> 00:09:42.043
Then lock down all six

00:09:42.043 --> 00:09:44.552
steps, before you deposit anything.

00:09:44.552 --> 00:09:46.859
Maria's twenty minutes shows exactly what

00:09:46.859 --> 00:09:48.397
that looks like in practice.

00:09:49.697 --> 00:09:52.742
This is education, not financial advice.

00:09:52.742 --> 00:09:56.147
Always verify you're on your exchange's real website

00:09:56.147 --> 00:09:59.296
before entering any password or code, and nobody

00:09:59.296 --> 00:10:01.930
from this program will ever ask for your

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.757
seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:06.057 --> 00:10:08.992
That's opening and securing an exchange account.

00:10:08.992 --> 00:10:11.787
Choose it well, lock it down, then deposit.

00:10:11.787 --> 00:10:13.831
Next up, Lesson 0.3: buying

00:10:13.831 --> 00:10:16.532
your first crypto without overpaying.
